 2. 🗳️ Senate stunner: Black Dem&#x27;s Deep South win 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sen.-elect Scott Colom (D-Miss.) speaks at a victory rally last night at Tougaloo College, a historically Black college just outside Jackson, Miss. Photo: Brandon Bell/Getty Images 🚨 Historic race call, 5:59 p.m. ET: WASHINGTON (AP) — Democrat Scott Colom wins election to U.S. Senate from Mississippi. 

 Democrat Scott Colom's upset of Republican Sen. Cindy Hyde-Smith makes him the first Democrat elected to the state's Senate delegation since 1982, Axios' Donica Phifer writes from Mississippi. 

 Colom also becomes Mississippi's first Black senator since Reconstruction. Why it matters: Colom's victory marks another extraordinary breakthrough for Democrats in the Deep South, extending the historic blue wave that handed the party control of both chambers of Congress.

 The win gives Democrats a 55–44 Senate majority , with one race still uncalled: Alaska, where Democrat Mary Peltola holds a narrow lead . The big picture: Colom's path to a historic U.S. Senate upset ran through the Mississippi Delta — tracing some of the most painful and defining chapters of Black American history, Axios' Russell Contreras writes. 

 It's where Emmett Till was abducted, Fannie Lou Hamer fought for the ballot and the blues became an American art form. Colom won 16 of the Delta's 18 counties, beating Hyde-Smith by about 38,000 votes in the region — roughly 13,000 more than the 2020 Democratic candidate's margin of victory there. Colom carried the whole state by only about 4,700 votes. Go deeper : How the Delta helped carry Scott Colom to Senate history. 

 
 
