 3. ⚡ Scoop: AOC shakes up staff before 2028 call 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (D-N.Y.) speaks to reporters outside the Capitol on Tuesday. Photo: Elizabeth Frantz/Reuters Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (D-N.Y.) is shaking up her team for the second time in the past few months as she prepares to make the biggest decision of her political life, Axios' Holly Otterbein and Alex Thompson write. 

 Why it matters: The overhaul comes as the democratic socialist leader debates whether to run for president in 2028 or for the seat of Senate Democratic Leader Chuck Schumer, which is up the same year. 🔭 Zoom in: Ocasio-Cortez has parted ways with her longtime digital fundraising firm, Middle Seat, according to three people familiar with the decision.

 She's brought on Aisle 518 Strategies, led by Tim Tagaris, who oversaw Bernie Sanders' record-breaking online fundraising strategy in two presidential campaigns. Middle Seat has been key to Ocasio-Cortez's operation. Over the past two years, she has paid the firm more than $6 million — about a quarter of her operation's disbursements during that period. AOC is close with Sanders, and if she decides to run for president, progressives say, she would be able to quickly staff up with his former advisers. 🥊 Reality check: Ocasio-Cortez's latest move doesn't necessarily signal that she's leaning toward a presidential run or a Senate bid.

 Tagaris' firm has helped power the small-dollar fundraising operation for California Gov. Gavin Newsom, who's widely expected to run for the White House. It has also worked with potential presidential contenders Sen. Mark Kelly (D-Ariz.), Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.) and former Vice President Kamala Harris. Share this story . 

 
 
