 4. ⚖️ Charted: Big-city crime decline 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : Major Cities Chiefs Association (MCCA). (Tulsa Police changed crime-reporting systems, which may affect year-over-year comparisons.) Chart: Russell Contreras/Axios Axios' Russell Contreras, who has closely tracked America's stunning post-pandemic crime drop, writes: 

 Homicides fell 15.2% across major U.S. police jurisdictions through the first nine months of 2026 compared with the same period last year, a new Major Cities Chiefs Association report found.

 Robberies fell 9.1%, rapes 3.5% and aggravated assaults 3.4%. 51 of the 66 large law enforcement agencies in the report logged overall declines. Between the lines: Not every city followed the trend.

 New York City was a major outlier, with more violent crime reported in all four categories, including 24.5% more homicides. In D.C., homicides fell 29.9%, but robberies surged 41.1%. Keep reading . 

 
 
