 5. 💰 Trump playbook tempts Dems 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Newly emboldened Democrats are warming to a move from President Trump's playbook: Turning the federal government into an investor in private companies, Axios' Amy Harder writes. 

 They're considering investments and aid for clean energy and energy infrastructure. 🎨 The big picture: The Biden administration spent billions subsidizing clean energy and semiconductor manufacturing. 

 The Trump administration has gone further by taking ownership stakes in companies ranging from Intel to critical-mineral producers. It has announced more than $30 billion in investments across nearly 50 deals involving government ownership and related financial arrangements, according to a Council on Foreign Relations tracker . 🔬 Zoom in: Jennifer Granholm, who served as President Biden's energy secretary, said at a gathering of energy and climate experts in Berkeley last month that "the Trump administration has widened the aperture for industrial policy."

 Granholm, a former Michigan governor, told us the government could take small ownership stakes in companies for national security or global competitiveness reasons. Keep reading ... Get Axios Future of Energy . 

 
 
