 6. 💵 Exclusive: Midterm mood swing 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Morning Consult. (Figures compare average daily sentiment readings over the seven days before each election with the five days after. Election Day is excluded.) Chart: Emily Peck/Axios The midterms triggered an unusually sharp partisan shift in how Americans feel about the economy, Axios' Emily Peck writes from Morning Consult data. 

 Why it matters: Partisan swings in consumer sentiment are typically far smaller after midterms than presidential elections. The latest numbers show just how much politics colors Americans' views of the economy. 🧮 By the numbers: Democratic consumer sentiment rose 6.5 points in the five days after the election compared with the week before, while Republicans' mood fell 6.4 points.

